From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 18:02:40 +0100
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: add the AVM FRITZ!Box 7583 VDSL

The FRITZ!Box 7583 VDSL is AVM hardware revision HW260, built on the
same GRX550 die as the FRITZ!Box 7580 and 7590. The vendor's hw260.dtsi
includes the shared GRX550 layer grx550_common.dtsi and the lamp file
leds-hw239.dtsi and adds nothing else. This file starts from the 7580's,
because that one needs the fewest edits. The switch wiring, USB, the
memory map with its reservations, the flash map and the LED enable stay
as they are in the 7580's file. The info, power and wlan lamps and the
dect key stay as well.

Six things differ from the 7580.

The revision is 260 and the sub-revision tag is 0, which is the key that
the vendor's Fritz_Box_HW260.dts gives this board. The boot image
carries a table of device-tree blobs keyed by hardware sub-revision. The
boot loader takes the entry that matches the unit's value or else a
lower one. The one unit on record reports 1 and boots the entry keyed 0.

RC2 has inbound byte swapping on, which is the value that the vendor's
grx_common.dtsi sets. The 7580's value of 0 comes from vrx318.dtsi,
which hw225.dtsi includes and hw260.dtsi does not. The part on RC2 has
the PCI ID 8086:09aa and no in-tree driver, so the comment names it by
its ID.

The front-panel controller's shift clock is no longer set, which leaves
the driver's 25 MHz default that grx550_common.dtsi asks for. The 7580's
3.125 MHz comes from hw225.dtsi, which hw260.dtsi does not include.

Pin 4 of the front-panel controller drives the internet lamp and pin 6
drives the Fon lamp, which is the order that leds-hw239.dtsi gives and
the reverse of the 7580's.

The wlan key is on gpio1 0 rather than gpio0 7, as leds-hw239.dtsi gives
it. With the 7580's file, the wlan key reads as held down on the unit on
record, because gpio0 7 reads low there.

The third key keeps its line and its keycode and is now named connect,
as the 08.26 copy of leds-hw239.dtsi names it.

The comments follow those of seale_avm_fritz7590ax.dts, so in places
they say more than the 7580's. The memory comment states that the full
512 MiB is unsafe while a boot loader built for a 256 MiB board, such as
the GRX350's, runs on the second CPU. The vendor's ngi.c denies access
above the memory size that the second CPU's device tree declares. The
vendor's tree for this board's second CPU, Fritz_Box_HW0260.dts,
declares 512 MiB.

Tested on one FRITZ!Box 7583 VDSL by RAM boot, by an install to flash
from the RAM-booted image and by a sysupgrade. The tests used Ethernet
on one LAN port, and both radios ran as access points that a scan from a
second device saw. The flash partitions appear as described, UBI
attaches, the six lamps and the three keys register and both USB
controllers enumerate. Wi-Fi clients, the WAN jack, the other LAN ports,
USB devices, the lamps and the keys were not exercised.

Sources: hw260.dtsi, leds-hw239.dtsi, grx550_common.dtsi,
grx_common.dtsi, grx550_bootcore.dtsi, Fritz_Box_HW260.dts and
Fritz_Box_HW0260.dts under arch/mips/boot/dts/lantiq/avm/ and
arch/mips/lantiq/grx500_bootcore/ngi.c in the vendor's 08.26 GPL release
for the FRITZ!Box 7583 VDSL. vrx318.dtsi, hw225.dtsi and an older copy
of leds-hw239.dtsi come from the vendor's 07.29 GPL release for the
FRITZ!Box 7580. That older copy puts the three keys on the same lines
and calls the third one wps. grx350_bootcore.dtsi comes from the
vendor's 07.30 GPL release for the FRITZ!Box 7560. The flash map, the
memory size and the sub-revision come from the unit's boot-loader
environment. The environment sample on boxmatrix.info, page "FRITZ!Box
7583 VDSL" as archived on 2026-01-04, reads the same values. The boot
with the entry keyed 0 was observed on the unit. The PCI ID comes from
this kernel running on the unit, and the level of gpio0 7 comes from the
same kernel running with the 7580's file.

Link: https://web.archive.org/web/20260104084950/https://www.boxmatrix.info/wiki/FRITZ!Box_7583_VDSL
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile
index 556efe484752..79b01363c840 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/Makefile
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@
 dtb-y	+= easy350_anywan.dtb
 dtb-y	+= seale_avm_fritz7560.dtb
 dtb-y	+= seale_avm_fritz7580.dtb
+dtb-y	+= seale_avm_fritz7583_vdsl.dtb
 dtb-y	+= seale_avm_fritz7590.dtb
 dtb-y	+= seale_avm_fritz7590ax.dtb
 dtb-y	+= seale_avm_fritz7590ax_v2.dtb
diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7583_vdsl.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7583_vdsl.dts
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..47b79a91b6f5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7583_vdsl.dts
@@ -0,0 +1,338 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+// Copyright (C) 2026 Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+/*
+ * The AVM FRITZ!Box 7583 VDSL is hardware revision HW260 on the GRX550 die
+ * for HWSubRevision 0 and 1.
+ * This file holds only its board-specific data. The SoC is described in
+ * xrx500.dtsi, the board family in seale_avm.dtsi and the die variant in
+ * seale_avm_grx550.dtsi.
+ */
+/dts-v1/;
+
+#include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/input/input.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/leds/common.h>
+
+#include "seale_avm_grx550.dtsi"
+
+/ {
+	model = "AVM FRITZ!Box 7583 VDSL";
+	compatible = "avm,fritz7583-vdsl", "intel,xrx500";
+
+	/*
+	 * The boot image carries a table of device-tree blobs keyed by
+	 * hardware sub-revision, and the boot loader looks the unit's value
+	 * up in it or takes a lower entry. This tree takes 0, the vendor's
+	 * own key for this board.
+	 */
+	avm-hw-revision {
+		compatible = "avm,avm_hw_revision";
+		revision = "260";
+		subrevision = "0";
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * The node covers the full 512 MiB DDR part at PHYS_OFFSET.
+	 *
+	 * Only the first half of it has an uncached alias. Above
+	 * PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M, the KSEG1 alias of a page is a register
+	 * address rather than DRAM. ZONE_DMA is sized to the window, and
+	 * dma-direct takes every uncached coherent allocation from there.
+	 * Holding the bound therefore does not cost the board half its
+	 * memory. Cached access is good across the whole node, since KSEG0
+	 * covers all of it.
+	 *
+	 * The full width is safe only while no boot loader built for a
+	 * 256 MiB board runs on the second CPU. Such a loader denies fabric
+	 * access above PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M, which is the upper half of this
+	 * node. A kernel shipped in a single-kernel container leaves the
+	 * second CPU's boot loader unstarted.
+	 */
+	memory@20000000 {
+		device_type = "memory";
+		reg = <0x20000000 0x20000000>;
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * These aliases name the boot, failsafe and upgrade lamps for
+	 * userspace. Without them, the boot-progress indication is silently
+	 * absent.
+	 */
+	aliases {
+		serial0 = &asc0;
+
+		led-boot = &led_power;
+		led-running = &led_power;
+		led-failsafe = &led_info_red;
+		led-upgrade = &led_info;
+		led-wifi = &led_wlan;
+	};
+
+	chosen {
+		stdout-path = "serial0:115200n8";
+		/*
+		 * This property is unused while MIPS_CMDLINE_BUILTIN_EXTEND
+		 * is selected. It is present so that a board that needs its
+		 * own arguments has somewhere to put them.
+		 */
+		bootargs = "";
+	};
+
+	/*
+	 * Any region below that a device reaches through the uncached alias
+	 * must stay under PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M. Above that address, the
+	 * alias reaches registers, not DRAM. The seed buffer at the end is
+	 * the one region that sits higher. It may, because it is no-map and
+	 * this kernel never reaches it at all.
+	 */
+	reserved-memory {
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <1>;
+		ranges;
+
+		/*
+		 * Crash persistence sits at the address the vendor's own
+		 * device tree uses for it. The vendor's node is named
+		 * ramoops@20500000, but its reg says 0x20100000. It takes
+		 * 1 MiB of the 4 MiB region below _text. The zone sizes fill
+		 * reg exactly, since fs/pstore/ram.c fails the whole probe if
+		 * they overflow it.
+		 */
+		ramoops@20100000 {
+			compatible = "ramoops";
+			reg = <0x20100000 0x100000>;
+			record-size = <0x4000>;
+			console-size = <0x80000>;
+			mem-type = <1>;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * This is the rest of the region that seale_avm.dtsi reserves.
+		 * The crash log above has taken its first megabyte.
+		 */
+		/delete-node/ avm-reserved@20100000;
+
+		avm-reserved@20200000 {
+			reg = <0x20200000 0x300000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * This region is owned by the second CPU's boot loader. The
+		 * vendor ships a device tree of its own for that core. The
+		 * region is no-map so that this kernel never maps another
+		 * CPU's memory.
+		 */
+		cpunet@2dfc0000 {
+			reg = <0x2dfc0000 0x40000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		bootcore@2e000000 {
+			reg = <0x2e000000 0x2000000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * This is a fixed-address seed buffer shared with the second
+		 * CPU. It is reserved no-map so that nothing here maps or
+		 * reads it.
+		 */
+		shm-rng-seed@3dfb0000 {
+			reg = <0x3dfb0000 0x1000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * The flash is a Toshiba BENAND part of 512 MiB with a 4 KiB page. Its
+ * geometry is self-identified. The partition map is the one the vendor boot
+ * loader installs, so a vendor recovery can hand the kernel back to the
+ * original firmware without remapping flash. The labels are ABI, because
+ * sysupgrade addresses partitions by label. They must not be renamed.
+ */
+&nand0 {
+	partitions {
+		compatible = "fixed-partitions";
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <1>;
+
+		/*
+		 * This partition holds the first-stage boot loader. It is
+		 * read-only, because overwriting it bricks the device past the
+		 * point of recovery without an external programmer.
+		 */
+		partition@0 {
+			label = "urlader";
+			reg = <0x0 0x100000>;
+			read-only;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * This is the AVM TFFS environment. It holds the only copy of
+		 * this unit's identity, its MAC addresses and serial number.
+		 * It is never written from Linux. Erasing it cannot be undone.
+		 */
+		partition@100000 {
+			label = "nand-tffs";
+			reg = <0x100000 0x400000>;
+			read-only;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * Two 8 MiB kernel slots follow, named by position. Which one
+		 * is active is boot-loader run-time state. The linux_fs_start
+		 * variable selects between them. No label may therefore claim
+		 * to be the active one.
+		 */
+		partition@500000 {
+			label = "kernel0";
+			reg = <0x500000 0x800000>;
+		};
+
+		partition@d00000 {
+			label = "kernel1";
+			reg = <0xd00000 0x800000>;
+		};
+
+		/* UBI holds rootfs and rootfs_data up to the 512 MiB mark. */
+		partition@1500000 {
+			label = "ubi";
+			reg = <0x1500000 0x1eb00000>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * RC0 and RC1 carry the two QCA9984 radios. The PCI domain numbers are
+ * ABI-visible, because userspace names the radios 0000:01:00.0 and
+ * 0001:01:00.0. RC2 carries a part with the PCI ID 8086:09aa, which has no
+ * in-tree driver. Inbound byte swapping stays on for this part.
+ */
+&pcie2 {
+	status = "okay";
+	intel,inbound-swap = <1>;
+	intel,outbound-swap = <0>;
+	reset-gpios = <&gpio0 16 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+};
+
+/*
+ * Front-panel lamps.
+ *
+ * The shift-register controller they hang off is a GRX550 fact and lives in
+ * seale_avm_grx550.dtsi. What is board data is which of its eight pins are
+ * populated and what each one drives. All six declared here are visible
+ * lamps.
+ *
+ * No shift clock rate is stated, which leaves the driver's 25 MHz default.
+ * That is the rate the vendor's GRX550 layer asks for, and the vendor's tree
+ * for this board does not lower it.
+ *
+ * The die layer's 250 kHz update rate selects a timer outside the SSO block,
+ * which nothing in this kernel programs. Unless that timer already runs, no
+ * output word ever reaches the shift register. A 10 Hz rate is the fastest
+ * that selects the SSO block's own divided clock instead.
+ */
+&ssogpio {
+	intel,sso-update-rate-hz = <10>;
+
+	/*
+	 * The child must be named "ssoled", because the driver matches on
+	 * the node name. The LEDs carry no label property, since colour and
+	 * function compose the ABI-visible LED name.
+	 */
+	ssoled {
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <0>;
+
+		/* The two halves of the bicolour info lamp. */
+		led_info: led@2 {
+			reg = <2>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = "info";
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 2 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		led_info_red: led@3 {
+			reg = <3>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_RED>;
+			function = "info";
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 3 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		led@4 {
+			reg = <4>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = "internet";
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 4 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		led_wlan: led@5 {
+			reg = <5>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = LED_FUNCTION_WLAN;
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 5 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		led@6 {
+			reg = <6>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = "fon";
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 6 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * The lamp is lit as soon as the driver probes. It is already
+		 * on when the kernel starts, and blinking it off mid-boot would
+		 * read as a failed boot. The line is active low.
+		 */
+		led_power: led@7 {
+			reg = <7>;
+			color = <LED_COLOR_ID_GREEN>;
+			function = LED_FUNCTION_POWER;
+			gpios = <&ssogpio 7 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+			default-state = "on";
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/* The keys are polled, because the GPIO banks publish no irq domain. */
+/ {
+	keys {
+		compatible = "gpio-keys-polled";
+		poll-interval = <100>;
+
+		button-dect {
+			label = "dect";
+			linux,code = <KEY_PHONE>;
+			gpios = <&gpio0 2 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		};
+
+		button-wlan {
+			label = "wlan";
+			linux,code = <KEY_RFKILL>;
+			gpios = <&gpio1 0 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		};
+
+		button-connect {
+			label = "connect";
+			linux,code = <KEY_WPS_BUTTON>;
+			gpios = <&gpio0 1 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+/*
+ * The master LED enable is a plain GPIO rather than an SSO pin. Nothing in
+ * this tree consumes it, which is why it is hogged. The whole panel is dark
+ * while it is low.
+ */
+&gpio1 {
+	led-enable-hog {
+		gpio-hog;
+		gpios = <11 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+		output-high;
+		line-name = "led-enable";
+	};
+};
